**Lorenz Transform**

We work in the \((1,-1,-1,-1)\) metric system. If covariant 4-vector \(p_{\mu}\) is equal \((E,p_{x},p_{y},p_{z})\) then contravariant 4-vector \(p^{\mu}\) is equal \((E,-p_{x},-p_{y},-p_{z})\). The Lorentz transform tensor as a function of \(\overrightarrow{\beta}=(\beta_{x},\beta_{y},\beta_{z})\) is given by:

\[\Lambda(\overrightarrow{\beta})=\left(\begin{array}{cccc}\gamma&-\gamma \beta_{x}&-\gamma\beta_{y}&-\gamma\beta_{z}\\ -\gamma\beta_{x}&1+\frac{(\gamma-1)\beta_{x}^{2}}{\beta^{2}}&\frac{(\gamma-1 )\beta_{x}\beta_{y}}{\beta^{2}}&\frac{(\gamma-1)\beta_{x}\beta_{z}}{\beta^{2}} \\ -\gamma\beta_{y}&\frac{(\gamma-1)\beta_{x}\beta_{y}}{\beta^{2}}&1+\frac{( \gamma-1)\beta_{x}^{2}}{\beta^{2}}&\frac{(\gamma-1)\beta_{y}\beta_{z}}{\beta^{ 2}}\\ -\gamma\beta_{z}&\frac{(\gamma-1)\beta_{x}\beta_{y}}{\beta^{2}}&\frac{(\gamma -1)\beta_{x}\beta_{y}}{\beta^{2}}&1+\frac{(\gamma-1)\beta_{z}^{2}}{\beta^{2}} \end{array}\right)\]

\[\text{where }\beta=\sqrt{\beta_{x}^{2}+\beta_{y}^{2}+\beta_{z}^{2}}\text{ and }\gamma=\frac{1}{\sqrt{1-\beta^{2}}}\]

The Lorentz transform of vector \(p^{\mu}\) is:

\[\left(p^{boost}\right)^{\mu}=\Lambda^{\mu}_{\nu}p^{\nu}\]

**Electron-Positron Pair Kinematics**

Let

\[p_{1}^{\mu}=(\left|\overrightarrow{p_{1}}\right|,\overrightarrow{p_{1}})=( p_{1},\overrightarrow{p}_{1\perp},p_{1z})=(p_{1},p_{1x},p_{1y},p_{1z})\text{ and }\]

\[p_{2}^{\mu}=(\left|\overrightarrow{p_{2}}\right|,\overrightarrow{p_{2}})=( p_{2},\overrightarrow{p}_{2\perp},p_{2z})=(p_{2},p_{2x},p_{2y},p_{2z})\]

be electron and positron 4-momenta (correspondingly) in the lab frame1. Lorentz transform to the center of mass frame is specified by the boost factor

Footnote 1: Electron/positron mass is ignored compared to the momentum.

\[\overrightarrow{\beta}^{CM}=-\frac{(\overrightarrow{p}_{1}+\overrightarrow {p}_{2})}{p_{1}+p_{2}}\]

In the center of mass frame electron/positron momenta are given by

\[\left(p_{1}^{\mu}\right)^{CM}=\Lambda^{\mu}_{\nu}\left(\overrightarrow{ \beta}^{CM}\right)\cdot p_{1}^{\nu}=\left(p_{1}^{CM},p_{1x}^{CM},p_{1y}^{CM},p_ {1z}^{CM}\right)\text{ and }\]

\[\left(p_{2}^{\mu}\right)^{CM}=\Lambda^{\mu}_{\nu}\left(\overrightarrow{ \beta}^{CM}\right)\cdot p_{2}^{\nu}=\left(p_{2}^{CM},p_{2x}^{CM},p_{2y}^{CM},p_ {2z}^{CM}\right)\]

Pair kinematical variables can be then expressed as:

\[p_{\perp}^{tot}=\left|\overrightarrow{p}_{1\perp}+\overrightarrow{p}_{2\perp}\right|\]\[Y^{pair}=-\ln\left(\frac{(p_{1}+p_{2})-(p_{1z}+p_{2z})}{(p_{1}+p_{2})+(p_{1z}+p_{2 z})}\right)\]

\[M_{inv}=\sqrt{p_{1}^{\mu}\cdot p_{2\mu}}\]

\[\varphi=\cos^{-1}\left(\frac{\left(\overrightarrow{p}_{1\perp}+ \overrightarrow{p}_{2\perp}\right)_{x}}{p_{\perp}^{tot}}\right)\]

\[\psi=\cos^{-1}\left(\frac{\left(\overrightarrow{p}_{1\perp}+\overrightarrow {p}_{2\perp}\right)\cdot\overrightarrow{p}_{2\perp}}{p_{\perp}^{tot}\cdot \left|\overrightarrow{p}_{2\perp}\right|}\right)\]

\[\theta^{\prime}=\left\{\begin{array}{c}\cos^{-1}\left(p_{1z}^{CM}/p_{1}^{ CM}\right),\;\mbox{if}\;p_{1z}^{CM}>0\\ 180^{\circ}-\cos^{-1}\left(p_{1z}^{CM}/p_{1}^{CM}\right),\;\mbox{if}\;p_{1z}^{ CM}<0\end{array}\right.\]

#### Euclidean Rotation

A Euclidean Rotation can be specified by an axis of rotation \(\overrightarrow{\nu}=(\nu_{x},\nu_{y},\nu_{z})\) and angle of rotation \(\phi\) around the axis \(\overrightarrow{\nu}\). Then a transformation matrix \(R\) is

\[R(\overrightarrow{\nu},\phi)=\left(\begin{array}{ccc}\nu_{x}^{2}+(1-\nu_{x }^{2})\cos\phi&\nu_{x}\nu_{y}(1-\cos\phi)-\nu_{z}\sin\phi&\nu_{x}\nu_{z}(1- \cos\phi)+\nu_{y}\sin\phi\\ \nu_{x}\nu_{y}(1-\cos\phi)+\nu_{z}\sin\phi&\nu_{y}^{2}+(1-\nu_{y}^{2})\cos \phi&\nu_{y}\nu_{z}(1-\cos\phi)-\nu_{x}\sin\phi\\ \nu_{x}\nu_{z}(1-\cos\phi)-\nu_{y}\sin\phi&\nu_{y}\nu_{z}(1-\cos\phi)+\nu_{x} \sin\phi&\nu_{z}^{2}+(1-\nu_{z}^{2})\cos\phi\end{array}\right)\]

A rotation of vector \(\overrightarrow{a}=(a_{x},a_{y},a_{z})\) can be specified as

\[\overrightarrow{a}^{\mbox{\scriptsize rotated}}=R\cdot\overrightarrow{a}\]

#### Effects of Photon Polarization

Cross-section formulae (2.5) and (2.6) assume that the photons in the center of mass frame are circularly polarized. If this is not the case, we need to distinguish two cross-sections: scalar cross-sections \(\sigma^{s}\) (two photon polarizations are parallel) and pseudoscalar cross-section \(\sigma^{ps}\) (perpendicular polarizations). For two photons in the lab frame with momenta \(\overrightarrow{k_{1}}=(0,0,\omega_{1})\), \(\overrightarrow{k_{2}}=(0,0,\omega_{2})\) and polarizations \(\overrightarrow{\epsilon_{1}}=(\overrightarrow{\epsilon_{1\perp}},0)\), \(\overrightarrow{\epsilon_{2}}=(\overrightarrow{\epsilon_{2\perp}},0)\) the total cross-section is then[15]:

\[\sigma\left(\omega_{1},\omega_{2},\overrightarrow{\epsilon_{1}}, \overrightarrow{\epsilon_{1}}\right)=\left(\overrightarrow{\epsilon_{1}} \cdot\overrightarrow{\epsilon_{1}}\right)^{2}\sigma^{s}\left(\omega_{1}, \omega_{2}\right)+\left(\overrightarrow{\epsilon_{1}}\times\overrightarrow{ \epsilon_{1}}\right)^{2}\sigma^{ps}\left(\omega_{1},\omega_{2}\right)\] (A.1)

where \(\sigma^{s}\) and \(\sigma^{ps}\) are given by[15]:

\[\sigma^{s}_{\gamma\gamma\to e^{+}e^{-}}(s)\!=\!8\pi\alpha^{2}\frac{1}{s} \left(1\!+\!\frac{4m^{2}}{s}\!-\!\frac{12m^{4}}{s^{2}}\right)\ln\left(\frac{ \sqrt{s}}{2m}\!+\!\sqrt{\frac{s}{4m^{2}}-1}\right)\!+\!4\pi\alpha^{2}\left( \frac{1}{s}\!+\!\frac{6m^{2}}{s^{2}}\right)\sqrt{1\!-\!\frac{4m^{2}}{s}}\;\mbox {and}\]\[\sigma^{ps}_{\gamma t\to e^{+}e^{-}}(s)=8\pi\alpha^{2}\frac{1}{s}\left(1+\frac{4m^{ 2}}{s}-\frac{4m^{4}}{s^{2}}\right)\ln\left(\frac{\sqrt{s}}{2m}+\sqrt{\frac{s}{4 m^{2}}-1}\right)+4\pi\alpha^{2}\left(\frac{1}{s}+\frac{2m^{2}}{s^{2}}\right)\sqrt{1- \frac{4m^{2}}{s}}\]

In the range of center of mass energies where STAR can observe \(e^{+}e^{-}\) pairs, the two cross-sections are approximately equal. In this case, the expression (A.1) evaluates to

\[\sigma\left(\omega_{1},\omega_{2},\overrightarrow{\epsilon_{1}}, \overrightarrow{\epsilon_{1}}\right)=\left[\left(\overrightarrow{\epsilon_{1} }\cdot\overrightarrow{\epsilon_{1}}\right)^{2}+\left(\overrightarrow{ \epsilon_{1}}\times\overrightarrow{\epsilon_{1}}\right)^{2}\right]\cdot \sigma(s)=\sigma(s)\]

since polarization vectors have unit length (\(\left|\overrightarrow{\epsilon}\right|=1\)). Therefore we do not need to consider two cases of polarization separately.

#### Two-Photon Luminosity

We start with an expression defining the two-photon luminosity

\[\sigma_{AuAu\to AuAu+e^{+}e^{-}}=\iint\frac{dL_{\gamma t}}{dM_{im}dY}\cdot \sigma_{\gamma t\to e^{+}e^{-}}\left(M_{im},Y\right)dM_{im}dY\] (A.2)

on the other hand, \(\sigma_{AuAu\to AuAu+e^{+}e^{-}}\) can be computed directly from photon densities (2.8) :

\[\sigma=\iiint\sigma_{\gamma t}\left(M_{im}\left(\omega_{1},\omega_{2}\right),Y\left(\omega_{1},\omega_{2}\right)\right)\frac{d^{2}n_{1}}{d^{2}\overrightarrow {b}_{1}}\left(\overrightarrow{b}_{1},\omega_{1}\right)\frac{d^{2}n_{2}}{d^{2 }\overrightarrow{b}_{2}}\left(\overrightarrow{b}_{2},\omega_{2}\right) \frac{d\omega_{1}}{\omega_{1}}\frac{d\omega_{2}}{\omega_{2}}d^{2} \overrightarrow{b}_{1}d^{2}\overrightarrow{b}_{2}\]

\[=\iint\sigma_{\gamma t}\left(\omega_{1},\omega_{2}\right)\cdot\frac{F_{ \gamma t}\left(\omega_{1},\omega_{2}\right)}{\omega_{1}\omega_{2}}\cdot d \omega_{1}d\omega_{2}\] (A.3)

Using the change of variable theorem, we can convert integral in Equation (A.3) into the integral over variables \(\left(M_{inv},Y\right)\) using the conversion \(\left(\omega_{1},\omega_{2}\right)=\left(M_{inv}\exp(Y)\right/2,M_{inv}\exp(-Y )/2):\)

\[\sigma=\iint\sigma_{\gamma t}\left(\omega_{1}\left(M_{inv},Y\right),\omega_{2} \left(M_{inv},Y\right)\right)\cdot\frac{F_{\gamma t}\left(\omega_{1}\left(M_{ inv},Y\right),\omega_{2}\left(M_{inv},Y\right)\right)}{\omega_{1}\left(M_{ inv},Y\right)\cdot\omega_{2}\left(M_{inv},Y\right)}\cdot\left|\frac{\partial \left(\omega_{1},\omega_{2}\right)}{\partial\left(M_{inv},Y\right)}\right|dM_{ inv}dY\]

where the Jacobian \(\left|\frac{\partial\left(\omega_{1},\omega_{2}\right)}{\partial\left(M_{inv},Y \right)}\right|\) is equal to:

\[\left|\frac{\partial\left(\omega_{1},\omega_{2}\right)}{\partial\left(M_{inv},Y\right)}\right|=\left|\begin{array}{cc}\frac{\partial\omega_{1}}{\partial M _{inv}}&\frac{\partial\omega_{1}}{\partial Y}\\ \frac{\partial\omega_{2}}{\partial M_{inv}}&\frac{\partial\omega_{2}}{\partial Y }\end{array}\right|=\left|\begin{array}{cc}\frac{\exp(Y)}{2}&\frac{M_{inv} \exp(Y)}{2}\\ \frac{\exp(-Y)}{2}&\frac{-M_{inv}\exp(-Y)}{2}\end{array}\right|=\frac{M_{inv}}{2}\]

Thus, Equation (A.3) can be re-written as:

\[\sigma_{AuAu\to AuAu+e^{+}e^{-}}=\iint\frac{F_{\gamma t}\left(\omega_{1} \left(M_{im},Y\right),\omega_{2}\left(M_{inv},Y\right)\right)}{M_{inv}^{2}/4} \cdot\frac{M_{inv}}{2}\cdot\sigma_{\gamma t\to e^{+}e^{-}}\left(M_{inv},Y \right)\cdot dM_{inv}dY\]

Comparing this to the Equation (A.2) we can conclude

\[\frac{dL_{\gamma t}}{dM_{inv}dY}=\frac{2F_{\gamma t}\left(\omega_{1}\left(M_{ inv},Y\right),\omega_{2}\left(M_{inv},Y\right)\right)}{M_{inv}}\]